"""KPI9: buys for one shopper inside an ORO store world.

Built on the public YesYesYes agent (race-16 top agent), itself built on Know Thyself v1 and KYO v6. A light model walks
the store with the world's own tools; code keeps the ledger, questions the shopper and stops wasted steps; a heavy model
reads the request, plans the questions and checks the cart before an order and the evidence before a decline.

Changes from YesYesYes:
- K1 Interviewer. Each message names up to three properties of one item, likeliest first; one property per message once
  the shopper objects to several at once, or when the request asks for one question at a time. The model labels every
  reply (value, pointer, no preference, not understood, too many) and what it speaks to; the next message follows from
  that: new properties after a "no preference", the list's later properties kept at the back of the queue. Candidate
  properties come from the keys the item's listings carry, screened by value shape (how many listings carry a key, how
  many values it shows, identifiers, free text); there are no word lists. Bundles with more items than messages share
  the first messages between items and keep the last for one item alone.
- K2 The answers are used at once: requirements, "does not mind", and a search with the new value whenever steps allow.
- K3 A refused order is re-planned in code: the cart's lines are re-read, each change in the note gets its own rule
  (sold out, price change, another listing cheaper, a need dropped or added, a new budget), and steps are kept for it.
- K4 A spending ceiling per task, checked against each call's worst-case price before it is sent, keeps a whole run
  well inside the per-run inference cap; heavy calls are fewer and smaller.
- K5 General throughout: the attribute word lists and the regex compatibility parser are gone (a light model reads
  whether two listings work together); a quiet ending keeps requested alternatives; a step limit is taken from the
  request only when the request states it.
"""
from __future__ import annotations
import json
import random
import re
import time
